  "abstract": "Periodic Collatz exponent patterns define local valuation reserves that are consumed during a block and replenished when the reference pattern changes. We determine the exact law and serial dependence of these adaptive charges for canonical blocks with exponent word $(1^{r-1},s+1)$. Under normalized odd $2$-adic Haar measure, consecutive block types are independent with probability $2^{-(r+s)}$, but the selected charges are not independent. At an ordinary block boundary the charge is zero with probability $1/9$ and has mass $h(3h-1)/(18\\,2^h)$ at every positive integer $h$. We obtain a closed covariance formula that is strictly negative at every nonzero lag. Although the marginal variance is $68/9$, an exact coboundary identity gives cumulative variance rate $4$, a functional central limit theorem, and an explicit finite concentration comparison. Counting only genuine reference changes instead gives mean $9/2$ and central-limit variance $807/196$, distinct from the conditional marginal variance $25/4$. We also provide a quantitative total-variation comparison with uniformly sampled positive odd integers using an exact negative-binomial precision budget. It yields a simultaneous integer-ensemble central limit theorem at block horizons tending to infinity strictly below $(1/4)\\log_2 N$, where $N$ is the number of source integers. Independent rational checkers audit the distributions, omitted tails, covariances, and binary-cylinder resolution. These results calibrate adaptive-reserve models; they provide no universal descent bound, no first-descent-conditioned transport, and no resolution of Collatz.",
